# EVL-105 direct CPU serving experiment

Use the locally approved Qwen3-0.6B artifact inventory and the pinned vLLM
0.29.0 CPU image used in the existing gateway qualification. The upstream
[serving guide](https://docs.vllm.ai/en/v0.29.0/serving/online_serving/)
provides the actual Chat Completions endpoint. The [benchmark CLI](https://docs.vllm.ai/en/v0.29.0/benchmarking/cli/)
separates completed throughput and end-to-end client latency. A short local
standard-library client preserves the measurements without the benchmark CLI's
dataset loader or a separate tokenizer configuration. The [CPU instructions](https://docs.vllm.ai/en/v0.29.0/getting_started/installation/cpu/)
cover the CPU thread and KV-cache controls. Use the exact same model,
request settings and four-CPU container limit at both concurrency levels.

[vLLM](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/blob/v0.29.0/LICENSE) and the
[pinned Qwen model](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B/blob/c1899de289a04d12100db370d81485cdf75e47ca/LICENSE)
are Apache-2.0, permitting offline commercial use and redistribution subject
to their notices. The preapproved image is used as-is. Existing PyTorch,
Transformers, tokenizers and safetensors licenses are documented in the EVL-106
research. The measurement client uses CPython under the PSF license and
repository-authored synthetic prompts. No new dependency or artifact is shipped.

Bind vLLM to loopback inside a network-none container and issue nonstreaming
requests from inside that namespace. Preverify the nine model files and reject
malformed completions, non-200 status, and inconsistent usage. Include warm-up
separately, retain elapsed time to full body and reported prompt and completion
tokens for every measured request. Recalculate both percentiles and rates from
the archived samples. Keep retries and startup separate from measurements.
No GPU, gateway admission, receipt, signed usage or cost is observed on this
path. Report a small CPU-only serving sample without a capacity claim.
